#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151954 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152163 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6c5f369-1196-5ace-9450-815a4ee2cc8f" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extended Euclidean Algorithm When the Two Numbers are Coprime"}] :question/id #uuid "df4647c2-73f7-57eb-b013-68d7f170266a" :question/math-academy-id "e-6496" :question/problem "Using the extended Euclidean algorithm, compute integers $u$ and $v$ such that $36u + 19v = \\text{gcd}(36,19).$ What is the value of $u+v?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}36 & = & 19\\cdot 1 & + & 17 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 19 & = & 17\\cdot 1 & + & 2 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 17 & = & 2\\cdot 8 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{gcd}(19,36) =1.$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, up to (and including) the equation where the greatest common divisor appeared as a remainder, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}17 & =36-19\\cdot 1 \\\\ 2 & =19-17 \\\\ 1 & =17-2\\cdot 8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =17-2\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =17-(19-17)\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =17\\cdot 9-19\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =[36-19]\\cdot 9-19\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =36\\cdot 9-19\\cdot 17 \\\\  & =36\\cdot 9+19\\cdot (-17)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $u=9,v=-17,$ and we can write\n\n$$\n1 = 36\\cdot \\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{9}  + 19\\cdot (\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{-17}) .\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nu+v=9+(-17)=-8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151981 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152184 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4caf9fc6-9247-5807-84de-208b731116e7" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extended Euclidean Algorithm When the Two Numbers are Not Coprime"}] :question/id #uuid "ed22bd9f-4dc9-528f-890f-2a81c4d353cd" :question/math-academy-id "e-6763" :question/problem "Using the extended Euclidean algorithm, compute integers $u$ and $v$ such that $30u + 8v = \\text{gcd}(30,8).$ What is the value of $u+v?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}30 & = & 8\\cdot 3 & + & 6 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 8 & = & 6\\cdot 1 & + & 2 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 6 & = & 2\\cdot 3 & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{gcd}(30,8) =2.$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, up to (and including) the equation where the greatest common divisor appeared as a remainder, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =30-8\\cdot 3 \\\\ 2 & =8-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =8-6 \\\\  & =8-(30-8\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =8\\cdot 4-30 \\\\  & =30\\cdot (-1)+8\\cdot 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $u=-1$ and $v=4.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nu+v=(-1)+4=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152184 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4caf9fc6-9247-5807-84de-208b731116e7" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extended Euclidean Algorithm When the Two Numbers are Not Coprime"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d4f7dfc-8c4b-5211-9ba9-17a6891ea4ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410953} {:answer/id #uuid "63787b7d-6757-5dd4-91d6-26ccae608ec1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410959} {:answer/id #uuid "6a9deb84-cae9-5549-80ed-f43cf3630291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410961} {:answer/id #uuid "8770928e-3c48-5020-8e38-8f716f8da09b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410965} {:answer/id #uuid "d4d4d5e8-df36-5a4a-b0d6-c06fc976cb01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410980}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d4f7dfc-8c4b-5211-9ba9-17a6891ea4ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410953} :answer-field/id #uuid "654d3597-5d7f-5384-b78b-7de4cca48b0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410989}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "321cfd56-0a7b-5b4f-978f-55f6493e6553" :question/math-academy-id "q-103236" :question/problem "Using the extended Euclidean algorithm, compute integers $u$ and $v$ such that $80u+35v=\\text{gcd}(80,35).$ What is the value of $u+v?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}80 & = & 35\\cdot 2 & + & 10 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 35 & = & 10\\cdot 3 & + & 5 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 10 & = & 5\\cdot 2 & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{gcd}(80,35)=5.$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =80-35\\cdot 2 \\\\ 5 & =35-10\\cdot 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =35-10\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =35-[80-35\\cdot 2]\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =35\\cdot 7-80\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =80\\cdot (-3)+35\\cdot 7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $u=-3$ and $v=7.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nu+v=(-3)+7=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410994 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152163 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6c5f369-1196-5ace-9450-815a4ee2cc8f" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extended Euclidean Algorithm When the Two Numbers are Coprime"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "130c9d5f-a494-5201-9007-5c05a96b42e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410947} {:answer/id #uuid "99ed6f58-98ec-51a0-9b38-3aadb20ec075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410968} {:answer/id #uuid "a5dcecfd-caea-541f-89f9-80edbb8f9da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410970} {:answer/id #uuid "a7c33fe1-1424-57a7-919e-e81ecac030fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410971} {:answer/id #uuid "ab1be111-0abb-58a5-9ac8-dcec925bc461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410972}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99ed6f58-98ec-51a0-9b38-3aadb20ec075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410968} :answer-field/id #uuid "549bec43-84e5-5d4d-8a01-c69248977616" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "32ff23bf-0d8e-550e-a2d3-333ad056d2c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-103258" :question/problem "Using the extended Euclidean algorithm, compute integers $u$ and $v$ such that $71u+29v=\\text{gcd}(71,29).$ What is the value of $u+v?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}71 & = & 29\\cdot 2 & + & 13 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 29 & = & 13\\cdot 2 & + & 3 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 13 & = & 3\\cdot 4 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nAs a result, $\\text{gcd}(71,29)=1.$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13 & =71-29\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & =29-13\\cdot 2 \\\\ 1 & =13-3\\cdot 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =13-3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =13-[29-13\\cdot 2]\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =13\\cdot 9-29\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =[71-29\\cdot 2]\\cdot 9-29\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =71\\cdot 9-29\\cdot 22 \\\\  & =71\\cdot 9+29\\cdot (-22)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $u=9$ and $v=-22.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nu+v=9+(-22)=-13.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410992 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152163 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6c5f369-1196-5ace-9450-815a4ee2cc8f" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extended Euclidean Algorithm When the Two Numbers are Coprime"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "363673d5-c3de-5c37-9ff1-f75b214ea273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410951} {:answer/id #uuid "82dc0705-3455-5756-8077-3a8e52dbc00a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410964} {:answer/id #uuid "8ab7acfd-c82b-5366-8f54-78640652a1e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410966} {:answer/id #uuid "b972d2a5-b19e-58c7-8b17-87752a454b23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410975} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a34ce9-0780-5e34-82bf-d50c72d63cf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410982}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7a34ce9-0780-5e34-82bf-d50c72d63cf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410982} :answer-field/id #uuid "4978dce7-3224-5493-bd63-afe2920d0881" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c3ce546-4615-54b8-9d08-ac0c684680c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-103265" :question/problem "Using the extended Euclidean algorithm, compute integers $u$ and $v$ such that $55u+24v=\\text{gcd}(55,24).$ What is the value of $u+v?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}55 & = & 24\\cdot 2 & + & 7 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 24 & = & 7\\cdot 3 & + & 3 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 7 & = & 3\\cdot 2 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{gcd}(55,24)=1.$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7 & =55-24\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & =24-7\\cdot 3 \\\\ 1 & =7-3\\cdot 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =7-3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =7-[24-7\\cdot 3]\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =7\\cdot 7-24\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =[55-24\\cdot 2]\\cdot 7-24\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =55\\cdot 7-24\\cdot 16 \\\\  & =55\\cdot 7+24\\cdot (-16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $u=7$ and $v=-16.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nu+v=7+(-16)=-9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152752 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152163 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6c5f369-1196-5ace-9450-815a4ee2cc8f" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extended Euclidean Algorithm When the Two Numbers are Coprime"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a7e30f3-8d4f-5e6c-9301-12bd11a35aa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410952} {:answer/id #uuid "61dc9228-8279-5741-964d-3